---
import EvidenceBundleInspector from "../components/EvidenceBundleInspector.astro";
import HeaderInspector from "../components/HeaderInspector.astro";
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Check supplied security evidence"
  description="Review security headers and other evidence you have already collected without sending it to ControlCurrent."
>
  <header class="shell page-header">
    <span class="eyebrow">No website scan or upload</span>
    <h1>Check security information you have already collected</h1>
    <p>
      Paste a set of response headers, or load a structured evidence file covering several pages.
      ControlCurrent checks whether expected browser protections appear and whether the supplied
      pages disagree. Everything is analysed on this device. It does not visit your website or prove
      that the protections work in production.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="shell">
    <HeaderInspector />
    <details class="advanced-assessment">
      <summary>
        <span>Advanced: review evidence from several pages and signed reports</span>
        <small>
          Use this only when you already have a structured evidence file or an approved CI report.
        </small>
      </summary>
      <div class="advanced-assessment__content">
        <EvidenceBundleInspector />
        <aside class="content-card">
          <span class="eyebrow">Optional advanced check</span>
          <h2>Confirm who produced a report in continuous integration (CI)</h2>
          <p>
            The command-line tool can check a digital signature added by an approved CI workflow.
            This can confirm that a particular workflow signed an unchanged report for the stated
            deployment.
          </p>
          <p>
            The <code>verify-evidence</code> command checks the signature, signing certificate, workflow
            identity, report fingerprint, and the rules chosen by your organisation. The smaller result
            does not retain the full signed bundle or certificate.
          </p>
          <p>
            A valid signature proves who signed that exact report. It does not prove that the
            original information was complete, accurate, or representative of the live website.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</Layout>
